    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey im studying for the security+ exam and im a bit stuck. if a password is already being hashed, why do we even need to salt it? doesnt the hash already make it unique? can u explain how salting actually helps against things like rainbow tables?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Hashed passwords, even when they look unique, can be vulnerable to attacks like rainbow tables, which are pre-computed lists of hashes for common passwords. Salting is crucial because it adds a unique, random string of data to each password *before* it gets hashed. This means that even if two users choose the exact same password, their resulting hashes will be completely different, which defeats the efficiency of rainbow tables. It forces an attacker to calculate a unique hash for every single password guess, making brute-forcing much slower.",
      "source": "target",
