    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. so the salt basically makes sure that someone cant just use a single pre-computed table to crack everyone at once. but what exactly is stored in the database alongside the hash? is the salt stored in plain text or is it hidden somehow?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, you've got the core idea right; the salt prevents the efficiency of those pre-computed tables. You *must* store the salt alongside the hash in the database, and it is stored in plain text. This is by design, because the salt needs to be available every time you need to verify a password during login. The server needs the specific salt associated with that user to re-hash the entered password and see if it matches the stored hash.",
